            <ArticleTitle>Influence of body mass index and weight on etanercept efficacy in patients with psoriasis: A retrospective study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>72-75</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the role of body mass index (BMI) and weight in the long-term efficacy of etanercept in patients with psoriasis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Medical records were retrospectively analysed. Extracted data included weight, BMI, comorbidities and psoriasis area severity index (PASI). Patients were stratified by weight (&lt;80 kg or ≥80 kg) and BMI (healthy, BMI 22 - 24.99 kg/m(2); overweight, BMI 25 - 29.99 kg/m(2); obese, BMI ≥30 kg/m(2)).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The study included 66 patients. Body weight had no effect on etanercept efficacy. There was a significant reduction in etanercept efficacy in obese patients (n = 12) compared with healthy weight (n = 33) or overweight (n = 21) patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Obesity has a negative effect on the efficacy of etanercept in psoriasis.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2016.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
